<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  polym2: bound ClickHouse's memory on a shared host.

  Mounted into /etc/clickhouse-server/config.d/ by docker-compose.infra.polym2.yml.
  Only meaningful for our private collection run; upstream's deployment has the
  box to itself and needs none of this.

  Background: on 2026-08-05 the kernel OOM-killed clickhouse-server 17 times in
  75 minutes. The host has 14 GB and runs a dozen unrelated services plus a KVM
  guest, so ClickHouse had roughly 4.6 GB to work with and was reaching 5.4 GB
  under merge load. The kill was `global_oom` rather than a cgroup limit, meaning
  the kernel was picking a victim host-wide and could as easily have taken
  something else down.

  Two defences, and the order matters:

    1. `max_server_memory_usage` makes ClickHouse refuse work it cannot afford,
       returning MEMORY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED instead of growing into the OOM killer.
       A failed insert is a visible error in the sink's [DATA-GAP] log; an
       OOM kill is a silent restart. Both lose data, but only one tells you.
    2. `mem_limit` in the compose overlay is the backstop, one step above this,
       so a miss here is contained to this container rather than the host.

  Careful with comments in this file: XML forbids a double hyphen inside one,
  and ClickHouse refuses to start on a malformed config rather than skipping it,
  which crash-loops the container.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <!-- 3.5 GiB. Sits below the 4 GB cgroup limit in the compose overlay so
         ClickHouse hits its own accounting first and can fail gracefully. -->
    <max_server_memory_usage>3758096384</max_server_memory_usage>

    <!-- Fewer concurrent merges means a lower peak. The insert path is cheap at
         our rate; it was merges over a multi-billion-row table that blew the
         budget, and the 6-hour TTL plus this together keep them small. -->
    <background_pool_size>8</background_pool_size>

    <!-- Do not start a merge that would need more than 10 GB of free disk. Not
         a memory setting, but the same class of protection: `/` is shared with
         the OS and every other service on this box. -->
    <max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool>10737418240</max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool>
</clickhouse>
